" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M14.7 6.3a4 4 0 0 0-5.4 5.4L3 18v3h3l6.3-6.3a4 4 0 0 0 5.4-5.4l-2.5 2.5-2.5-2.5z\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-clip\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><rect x=\"5\" y=\"4\" width=\"14\" height=\"17\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M9 4V3h6v1M9 10h6M9 14h6M9 18h3\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-check\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"m5 12.5 4.5 4.5L19 7.5\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-minus\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M6 12h12\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-info\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"9\"/><path d=\"M12 11v6M12 7.5v.5\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-star\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M12 2.5l2.9 6.1 6.6.8-4.9 4.6 1.3 6.6L12 17.3l-5.9 3.3 1.3-6.6-4.9-4.6 6.6-.8z\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-down\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M12 4v12M6 10l6 6 6-6M5 20h14\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-cert\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"4\" width=\"18\" height=\"12\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M7 8h10M7 11h6M15 16v5l2-1.5 2 1.5v-5\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-family\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><circle cx=\"7\" cy=\"6\" r=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"17\" cy=\"6\" r=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"M3 20c0-4 2-7 4-7s4 3 4 7M13 20c0-4 2-7 4-7s4 3 4 7\"/></symbol>\n</svg>\n\n", "header": "<header class=\"site-head\">\n    <a class=\"logo\" href=\"/en\" aria-label=\"Ocean Way, home\"><svg viewBox=\"2 2 116 116\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><defs><clipPath id=\"logo-clip\"><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"60\" r=\"47.01\"/></clipPath></defs><g clip-path=\"url(#logo-clip)\" fill=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"72.3\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"54.6\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"11\"/><path d=\"M28 59 C36 36 72 23.5 118 32 L118 51 C84 41 47 43.5 28 59Z\"/></g><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"60\" r=\"52.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"11\"/></svg></a>\n    <nav class=\"site-nav\" aria-label=\"Main\">\n      <a class=\"navl\" href=\"/en#courses\">Courses</a>\n      <a class=\"navl\" href=\"/en#passages\">Passages</a>\n      <a class=\"navl on\" href=\"/en/theory\">Theory</a>\n      <a class=\"navl\" href=\"/en#fleet\">Fleet</a>\n      <a class=\"navl\" href=\"#\">Prices</a>\n    </nav>\n    <div class=\"site-tools\">\n      <details class=\"lang\" translate=\"no\"><summary class=\"round\" aria-label=\"Language: English\">EN</summary><ul class=\"lang-menu\"><li><a href=\"/en/theory/buoyage\" hreflang=\"en\" lang=\"en\" aria-current=\"true\">English<span>EN</span></a></li><li><a href=\"/ru/theory/buoyage\" hreflang=\"ru\" lang=\"ru\">Русский<span>RU</span></a></li><li><a href=\"/de/theory/buoyage\" hreflang=\"de\" lang=\"de\">Deutsch<span>DE</span></a></li><li><a href=\"/es/theory/buoyage\" hreflang=\"es\" lang=\"es\">Español<span>ES</span></a></li></ul></details>\n      \n      \n      \n      \n      \n      \n      \n      \n      <button class=\"round\" type=\"button\" id=\"themeBtn\" aria-label=\"Theme\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-theme\"/></svg></button>\n      <button class=\"round\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Saved\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-heart\"/></svg></button>\n      <button class=\"round\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Account\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-user\"/></svg></button>\n    </div>\n  </header>", "main": "\n    <article>\n      <header class=\"art-head\">\n        <nav class=\"crumbs\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><ol><li><a href=\"/en\">Home</a></li><li><a href=\"/en/theory\">Theory</a></li><li aria-current=\"page\">Rules of the road</li></ol></nav>\n        <h1 class=\"display\">Buoys and marks: the IALA system</h1>\n        <p class=\"lead\">Buoys show where the channel runs and which side of a danger is safe. This guide covers the IALA marks you will meet in the Mediterranean, by day and at night.</p>\n        <div class=\"byline\"><span class=\"meta\" style=\"color:var(--ink)\">Ocean Way instructors</span><span class=\"meta\">Updated 1 Nov 2026</span><span class=\"meta\">6 min read</span><span class=\"lvl\">Bareboat skipper</span></div>\n      </header>\n      <div class=\"art-grid\">\n        <div class=\"art-body\">\n          <details class=\"art-mobile-toc band\"><summary>On this page<svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-chev\"/></svg></summary><ol class=\"toc-list\"><li><a href=\"#regions\">Regions A and B</a></li><li><a href=\"#lateral\">Lateral marks</a></li><li><a href=\"#cardinals\">Cardinal marks</a></li><li><a href=\"#isolated-safe\">Isolated danger and safe water</a></li><li><a href=\"#special\">Special and wreck marks</a></li><li><a href=\"#lights\">Reading lights</a></li><li><a href=\"#real-life\">Buoys in real life</a></li><li><a href=\"#practise\">Practise it</a></li></ol></details>\n          <div class=\"band summary\"><span class=\"kicker\">In short</span><ul><li>In Region A, coming in from sea, red can marks are left to port and green cones to starboard.</li><li>Cardinal topmarks point to the black on the mark. Pass on the side the mark is named after.</li><li>Cardinal lights follow a clock face: east 3 flashes, south 6 and a long flash, west 9, north continuous.</li><li>Buoys drag, go missing and may be unlit, so check every mark against the chart.</li></ul></div>\n          <section id=\"regions\">\n<h2>One system, two regions</h2>\n<p>Buoys and beacons (fixed marks on posts or rocks) follow the IALA Maritime Buoyage System, agreed worldwide. Every mark gives you four clues: its colour, its shape, its topmark (the small shape on top) and, at night, its light.</p>\n<p>The world is split into two regions, which differ only in the colours of the lateral marks that show the sides of a channel.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Region A.</b> Europe, the Mediterranean including Greece and Turkey, Africa, most of Asia, Australia and New Zealand. Coming in from sea, red marks are left to port.</li>\n<li><b>Region B.</b> North, Central and South America, Japan, South Korea and the Philippines. The lateral colours are reversed, so coming in from sea, red marks are left to starboard.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>All other marks are the same in both regions. Everything below describes Region A, where we sail.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"lateral\">\n<h2>Lateral marks: the sides of the channel</h2>\n<p>Lateral marks show the two edges of a channel, like the kerbs of a road.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Port-hand mark.</b> Red and can-shaped (a flat-topped cylinder), with a red can as topmark if one is fitted. Any light is red.</li>\n<li><b>Starboard-hand mark.</b> Green and cone-shaped, with a green cone pointing up as topmark if one is fitted. Any light is green.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Lateral lights may use any rhythm except two flashes followed by one, Fl(2+1), which is kept for preferred-channel marks.</p>\n<h3>Direction of buoyage</h3>\n<p>Port and starboard here mean your sides when you travel in the direction of buoyage. Near a harbour, river or estuary, that means coming in from seaward. Along open coasts it is set by the authorities and generally runs clockwise around the continents. Where it could be unclear, the chart shows it with an arrow symbol. Leaving harbour, everything reverses: you keep red marks to starboard.</p>\n<h3>Preferred-channel marks</h3>\n<p>Where a channel divides, a modified lateral mark shows the main branch. Its light is Fl(2+1), in the colour of the mark's main body.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Preferred channel to starboard.</b> A red can with one broad green band. Leave it to port and the main channel lies to starboard.</li>\n<li><b>Preferred channel to port.</b> A green cone with one broad red band. Leave it to starboard to stay in the main channel.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"cardinals\">\n<h2>Cardinal marks: which side is safe</h2>\n<p>A cardinal mark stands to the north, east, south or west of a danger such as a rock, shoal or wreck. Its name tells you the safe side: pass north of a north cardinal, east of an east cardinal, and so on. All cardinals are black and yellow, with two black cones as topmark, and any light is white.</p>\n<p>The topmark is the key to the colours. The black on the mark is where the cones point.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n<thead><tr><th>Mark</th><th>Topmark</th><th>Colours</th><th>Light</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>North cardinal</td><td>Both cones pointing up</td><td>Black above yellow</td><td>Q or VQ, continuous</td></tr>\n<tr><td>East cardinal</td><td>Cones base to base, points apart</td><td>Black with a broad yellow band</td><td>Q(3) 10s or VQ(3) 5s</td></tr>\n<tr><td>South cardinal</td><td>Both cones pointing down</td><td>Yellow above black</td><td>Q(6)+LFl 15s or VQ(6)+LFl 10s</td></tr>\n<tr><td>West cardinal</td><td>Cones point to point, points together</td><td>Yellow with a broad black band</td><td>Q(9) 15s or VQ(9) 10s</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"dgfig\"><div class=\"frame\"><svg class=\"dg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 470\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Four cardinal marks around a danger, each on the side named after it, with their colours, topmarks and quick-flashing light rhythms\"><line class=\"dg-ring\" x1=\"352.5\" y1=\"217.5\" x2=\"443.7\" y2=\"126.3\"/><line class=\"dg-ring\" x1=\"352.5\" y1=\"282.5\" x2=\"443.7\" y2=\"373.7\"/><line class=\"dg-ring\" x1=\"287.5\" y1=\"282.5\" x2=\"196.3\" y2=\"373.7\"/><line class=\"dg-ring\" x1=\"287.5\" y1=\"217.5\" x2=\"196.3\" y2=\"126.3\"/><path class=\"dg-nogo\" d=\"M290,256 C286,236 310,224 328,230 C350,234 354,254 342,268 C328,280 298,276 290,256Z\"/><text class=\"dg-label dg-muted\" x=\"320\" y=\"300\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Danger</text><line class=\"dg-water\" x1=\"286\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"354\" y2=\"130\"/><rect class=\"dg-blk\" x=\"307\" y=\"72.0\" width=\"26\" height=\"28.0\"/><rect class=\"dg-yel\" x=\"307\" y=\"100.0\" width=\"26\" height=\"28.0\"/><rect class=\"dg-outline\" x=\"307\" y=\"72\" width=\"26\" height=\"56\" rx=\"3\"/><line class=\"dg-mast\" x1=\"320\" y1=\"72\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"62\"/><polygon class=\"dg-cone\" points=\"311,62 329,62 320,49\"/><polygon class=\"dg-cone\" points=\"311,47 329,47 320,34\"/><text class=\"dg-label\" x=\"350\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"start\">North cardinal</text><text class=\"dg-label dg-muted\" x=\"350\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"start\">Q · pass north of it</text><line class=\"dg-water\" x1=\"476\" y1=\"280\" x2=\"544\" y2=\"280\"/><rect class=\"dg-blk\" x=\"497\" y=\"222.0\" width=\"26\" height=\"18.7\"/><rect class=\"dg-yel\" x=\"497\" y=\"240.7\" width=\"26\" height=\"18.7\"/><rect class=\"dg-blk\" x=\"497\" y=\"259.3\" width=\"26\" height=\"18.7\"/><rect class=\"dg-outline\" x=\"497\" y=\"222\" width=\"26\" height=\"56\" rx=\"3\"/><line class=\"dg-mast\" x1=\"510\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"510\" y2=\"212\"/><polygon class=\"dg-cone\" points=\"501,199 519,199 510,212\"/><polygon class=\"dg-cone\" points=\"501,197 519,197 510,184\"/><text class=\"dg-label\" x=\"510\" y=\"306\" text-anchor=\"middle\">East cardinal</text><text class=\"dg-label dg-muted\" x=\"510\" y=\"324\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Q(3) · pass east of it</text><line class=\"dg-water\" x1=\"286\" y1=\"440\" x2=\"354\" y2=\"440\"/><rect class=\"dg-yel\" x=\"307\" y=\"382.0\" width=\"26\" height=\"28.0\"/><rect class=\"dg-blk\" x=\"307\" y=\"410.0\" width=\"26\" height=\"28.0\"/><rect class=\"dg-outline\" x=\"307\" y=\"382\" width=\"26\" height=\"56\" rx=\"3\"/><line class=\"dg-mast\" x1=\"320\" y1=\"382\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"372\"/><polygon class=\"dg-cone\" points=\"311,359 329,359 320,372\"/><polygon class=\"dg-cone\" points=\"311,344 329,344 320,357\"/><text class=\"dg-label\" x=\"350\" y=\"380\" text-anchor=\"start\">South cardinal</text><text class=\"dg-label dg-muted\" x=\"350\" y=\"398\" text-anchor=\"start\">Q(6)+LFl · pass south of it</text><line class=\"dg-water\" x1=\"96\" y1=\"280\" x2=\"164\" y2=\"280\"/><rect class=\"dg-yel\" x=\"117\" y=\"222.0\" width=\"26\" height=\"18.7\"/><rect class=\"dg-blk\" x=\"117\" y=\"240.7\" width=\"26\" height=\"18.7\"/><rect class=\"dg-yel\" x=\"117\" y=\"259.3\" width=\"26\" height=\"18.7\"/><rect class=\"dg-outline\" x=\"117\" y=\"222\" width=\"26\" height=\"56\" rx=\"3\"/><line class=\"dg-mast\" x1=\"130\" y1=\"222\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"212\"/><polygon class=\"dg-cone\" points=\"121,212 139,212 130,199\"/><polygon class=\"dg-cone\" points=\"121,184 139,184 130,197\"/><text class=\"dg-label\" x=\"130\" y=\"306\" text-anchor=\"middle\">West cardinal</text><text class=\"dg-label dg-muted\" x=\"130\" y=\"324\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Q(9) · pass west of it</text></svg></div><figcaption class=\"disclaimer\">Cardinal marks around a danger, north at the top. Each mark sits on the side it is named after, so you pass on that side. The cones point to where the black is, and the white light flashes like a clock face: 3 at east, 6 at south, 9 at west.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>For the lights, think of a clock face. East is at three o'clock and gives three flashes. South is at six and gives six, then one long flash so it cannot be mistaken for three or nine. West is at nine and gives nine. North, at twelve, flashes without a break.</p>\n<aside class=\"note warn\"><b>Watch out</b><p>A cardinal mark tells you which side is safe, not how far the danger reaches. A shoal can extend some way beyond the mark, so give it a good berth and check the chart.</p></aside>\n</section>\n<section id=\"isolated-safe\">\n<h2>Isolated danger and safe water</h2>\n<p><b>Isolated danger mark.</b> Black with one or more broad red bands, and a topmark of two black balls, one above the other. It sits on or over a small danger, such as a single rock, with navigable water all around. Its light is white, a group of two flashes, Fl(2). Two balls, two flashes. Pass well clear on any side.</p>\n<p><b>Safe water mark.</b> Red and white vertical stripes, with a single red ball as topmark. It shows navigable water all around, often at the seaward end of a harbour approach or in mid-channel. Its light is white: isophase (Iso), occulting (Oc), one long flash every 10 seconds (LFl 10s) or Morse code A, Mo(A).</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"special\">\n<h2>Special marks and wreck buoys</h2>\n<p><b>Special marks.</b> Yellow, with a yellow X as topmark if one is fitted, and a yellow light if lit. They mark things such as swimming areas, cables and pipelines, outfalls, racing marks and fish farms. In summer, lines of small yellow buoys off Greek and Turkish beaches mark swimming zones. Keep out of them under power.</p>\n<p><b>Emergency wreck-marking buoy.</b> Blue and yellow vertical stripes, with a yellow upright cross (shaped like a plus sign) as topmark and a light alternating blue and yellow flashes. It marks a new wreck that is not yet on the chart, until the wreck is surveyed and permanently marked. Keep well clear.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"lights\">\n<h2>Reading lights on the chart</h2>\n<p>Beside each lit mark, the chart prints a short code, the light characteristic. It gives the rhythm, the colour and the period: the time in seconds from the start of one sequence to the start of the next.</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n<thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Name</th><th>What you see</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>F</td><td>Fixed</td><td>A steady light</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fl</td><td>Flashing</td><td>Flashes shorter than the dark between them</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fl(3)</td><td>Group flashing</td><td>Groups of three flashes, then a longer dark</td></tr>\n<tr><td>LFl</td><td>Long flashing</td><td>A flash lasting at least 2 seconds</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Q</td><td>Quick</td><td>50 or 60 flashes a minute</td></tr>\n<tr><td>VQ</td><td>Very quick</td><td>100 or 120 flashes a minute</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Iso</td><td>Isophase</td><td>Light and dark of equal length</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Oc</td><td>Occulting</td><td>Light longer than dark, with short eclipses</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mo(A)</td><td>Morse code A</td><td>A short flash, then a long one</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Colours are shown by letters: R red, G green, Y yellow, W white. No colour letter means white. A full entry such as Fl(2)R 6s 7m 4M reads: two red flashes every 6 seconds, from a light 7 metres high, visible for 4 nautical miles in clear weather. To time a light, watch two or three full cycles and count, or use a stopwatch.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"real-life\">\n<h2>Buoys in real life</h2>\n<p>A buoy is a clue to check, not a fact.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>They move and go missing.</b> A buoy can drag in a storm, break free or be removed for repair, and lights fail. Never steer from buoy to buoy without checking each one against the chart.</li>\n<li><b>Many are unlit.</b> A mark you passed easily by day may be invisible at night. Only marks with a light characteristic on the chart are lit.</li>\n<li><b>Identify before you rely.</b> Check colour, shape, topmark and light, then confirm your position by plotter, bearing or depth sounder.</li>\n<li><b>Small harbours have few marks.</b> In Greece and Turkey, many small harbours have no buoyed channel, just a light on each breakwater end: red to port and green to starboard as you enter. Isolated rocks are often marked by a beacon on the rock itself.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Enter an unfamiliar harbour in daylight when you can, and read the pilot book first.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"practise\">\n<h2>Practise it on the water</h2>\n<p>On our courses you name every mark on the approach to a new harbour before you reach it: from the chart first, then by eye, and at night by counting and timing its light. Night passages along the Turkish coast and among the Greek islands give you real cardinal and harbour lights to identify from the cockpit.</p>\n</section>\n\n          <div class=\"art-mobile-course\"><div class=\"band art-course\"><span class=\"kicker\">Practise it on</span><div class=\"card-title\">International Bareboat Skipper</div><p>Learn this on the water with an instructor, in a week living aboard.</p><a class=\"pill pill-primary pill-wide pill-sm\" href=\"/en/courses/bareboat-skipper\">See the course</a></div></div>\n        </div>\n        <aside class=\"art-side\" aria-label=\"On this page\"><span class=\"kicker\">On this page</span><ol class=\"toc-list\"><li><a href=\"#regions\">Regions A and B</a></li><li><a href=\"#lateral\">Lateral marks</a></li><li><a href=\"#cardinals\">Cardinal marks</a></li><li><a href=\"#isolated-safe\">Isolated danger and safe water</a></li><li><a href=\"#special\">Special and wreck marks</a></li><li><a href=\"#lights\">Reading lights</a></li><li><a href=\"#real-life\">Buoys in real life</a></li><li><a href=\"#practise\">Practise it</a></li></ol><div class=\"band art-course\"><span class=\"kicker\">Practise it on</span><div class=\"card-title\">International Bareboat Skipper</div><p>Learn this on the water with an instructor, in a week living aboard.</p><a class=\"pill pill-primary pill-wide pill-sm\" href=\"/en/courses/bareboat-skipper\">See the course</a></div></aside>\n      </div>\n    </article>\n    <section class=\"sec\" style=\"margin-top:96px\">\n      <div class=\"sec-head row-head\"><div><span class=\"kicker\">Theory</span><h2 class=\"h2\" style=\"margin-top:12px\">Keep reading</h2></div><div class=\"row-nav\"><a class=\"pill pill-quiet\" href=\"/en/theory\">All theory</a></div></div>\n      <div class=\"hscroll\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"More theory\"><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/right-of-way\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1605281317010-fe5ffe798166?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A motor yacht crossing open water\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Bareboat skipper</span><span class=\"card-title\">Right of way at sea: the rules skippers use daily</span><span class=\"card-meta\">The COLREGs a yacht skipper uses daily: lookout, safe speed, risk of collision, give-way rules for sail and power, overtaking, motorsailing and channels.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/navigation-lights\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1534447677768-be436bb09401?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A yacht under a starry night sky\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Bareboat skipper</span><span class=\"card-title\">Navigation lights and day shapes</span><span class=\"card-meta\">Masthead, side and stern lights, tricolour, anchor light and day shapes under COLREG Part C, plus how to read fishing boats, tows and ships at night.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/points-of-sail\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1540946485063-a40da27545f8?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A sailing yacht close-hauled on open water\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span><span class=\"card-title\">Points of sail: how a yacht sails close to the wind</span><span class=\"card-meta\">Learn the points of sail from close-hauled to running: the no-go zone, sail trim on each point, tacking and gybing, apparent wind and reading telltales.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/knots\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1563296291-14f26f10c20f?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A schooner with all sails set\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span><span class=\"card-title\">Six knots every crew member uses</span><span class=\"card-meta\">Six sailing knots every crew member needs: bowline, clove hitch, round turn and two half hitches, cleat hitch, figure-of-eight and reef knot, step by step.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/tacking-gybing\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1777880652269-ff1dd5e65fbb?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"Two sailors at the wheel under sail\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span><span class=\"card-title\">Tacking and gybing, step by step</span><span class=\"card-meta\">How to tack and gybe a sailing yacht: the calls, crew jobs, winches without riding turns, a controlled gybe, getting out of irons and tips for catamarans.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/reefing\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1777404380697-6b0725b148a7?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"Two crew working at the mast in a fresh breeze\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Bareboat skipper</span><span class=\"card-title\">Reefing: when and how to shorten sail</span><span class=\"card-meta\">When and how to reef a sailing yacht: signs it is time, slab reefing step by step, in-mast furling mains, rolling in the genoa, sail balance and shaking out.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a></div>\n    </section>\n  ", "footer": "<footer class=\"site-foot\">\n    <div class=\"foot-row\">\n      <span class=\"meta\">Ocean Way · Layout prototype. Prices, dates and people are sample content. Photos via Unsplash.</span>\n      <nav class=\"foot-links\"><a href=\"#\">About</a><a href=\"#\">Telegram</a><a href=\"#\">Privacy</a></nav>\n    </div>\n  </footer>", "scripts": ["\nconst root = document.documentElement;\ndocument.getElementById('themeBtn').addEventLi